<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Clement VIII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1536">1536</a>–<a href="/years/1605">1605</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ippolito Aldobrandini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Clement VIII reigned from 1592 to 1605. He absolved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> in 1595 and brought the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> under direct papal rule in 1598, refusing to recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-d-este-duke-of-modena">Cesare d'Este</a> as heir. He also issued the Clementine Vulgate and raised small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> such as Masserano to principalities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-clement-viii.jpg" alt="Pope Clement VIII" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gelindu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1280282369_clement-viii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ippolito Aldobrandini was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fano">Fano</a> in the Papal States on 24 February 1536, the son of Silvestro Aldobrandini and Lesa Deti, from a Florentine family. Like his father he became a canon lawyer and an Auditor of the Roman Rota, the highest ecclesiastical court of the Holy See. He was ordained a priest only on 31 December 1580, at forty-five. Sixtus V made him Cardinal-Priest of S. Pancrazio in 1585 and in 1588 sent him as legate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, where he won the gratitude of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> by securing the release of Archduke Maximilian. His confessor for thirty years was Saint Philip Neri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected on 30 January 1592 after a stormy conclave in which a minority of Italian cardinals refused to be dictated to by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, he took the name Clement VIII and aimed to free the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> from dependence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In November 1592 he published the Clementine Vulgate, which remained the official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> until the Nova Vulgata replaced it in 1979.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> on 25 July 1593, and in the autumn of 1595 Clement solemnly absolved him despite Spanish displeasure, ending the thirty years of religious war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-deste">Alfonso II d'Este</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, died childless on 27 October 1597, Clement resolved to attach Ferrara to the Papal States; Henry IV's threats deterred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the emperor from helping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-d-este-duke-of-modena">Cesare d'Este</a>, and a papal army entered Ferrara almost unopposed. In 1594 he began an alliance of Christian powers for the Long War against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Clement was severe in matters of order and belief. He had several noble troublemakers put to death, including Beatrice Cenci, who had murdered her father, probably after his repeated abuses, and he rejected many pleas for clemency. In 1599 the miller Menocchio was burned after trial by the Inquisition, and in 1600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giordano-bruno">Giordano Bruno</a> was burned at the stake, Clement taking part in the final phase of the trial. The bull Caeca et Obdurata of 1593 banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from living in the Papal States outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Ancona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>.</p><p>The jubilee of 1600 brought three million pilgrims. Crippled by gout in his last years, Clement died on 3 March 1605 and was buried in St Peter's; his remains were moved in 1646 to Santa Maria Maggiore. He left a reputation for prudence, munificence, ruthlessness and capacity for business.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Clement VIII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pope Clement VIII</b> refused to recognise his cousin Cesare, and in 1598 Ferrara passed to the Papal States.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ferrara">Duchy of Ferrara</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1598 <b>Pope Clement VIII</b> raised them to a principality held as a fief of the Holy See, giving the family sovereign status.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-masserano">Principality of Masserano</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fano">Fano</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.84,13.01&amp;year=1580" aria-label="Fano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4862 610 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Clement VIII"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.84,13.01&amp;year=1580">What was here in 1580? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
